-- Restyled: the restyled liftback, as data (docs/game/features/car-model.md "The restyled
-- model", T-287). The default car since T-287; the gray model (`Cars/Liftback`) is kept.
--
-- The same 1980s liftback as `Cars/Liftback`, remodelled in Blender from a detailed model of
-- the real car, exported as `assets/car-ae86/car-ae86-mid.fbx` (with `car-ae86-mid.json`) and
-- imported by the user into DR-Lane1 as `Workspace.car-ae86-mid` on 2026-11-17. Read out of
-- that place into this file so every place can build it: 142 mesh pieces, each
-- `<Part>_<Surface>`, in 8 static areas (`Body`, `BumperFront`, `BumperRear`, `Underside`,
-- `EngineBay`, `Engine`, `Cabin`, `Windows`: all the static body) and 54 groups, 36 moving
-- parts and 18 lamps. `Shared/Cars/MeshRig` turns it into a v2 `Shell` through
-- `Rig.assemble` (CONTRACTS.md "The car rig"); `Shared/CarModel` builds it as the body
-- `restyled`.
--
-- The same layout as `Cars/Liftback`: the game's car axes (X = the car's right, Y = up,
-- -Z = forward), in studs, origin ON THE GROUND midway between the axles. Wheelbase 9.6
-- (the game's), track 5.722, so it is built at scale 1.
--
--   surfaces  the export's starting look per surface (`car-ae86-mid.json`), and its `Paint`
--             role. `maps` are the SurfaceAppearance image ids the import uploaded for the
--             three textured surfaces (the tyre's tread is in its normal map). A game script
--             cannot write them, so they are also made ahead in `Cars/RestyledMaps.rbxmx`
--             (`mapsFolder`), one SurfaceAppearance per surface, which `MeshRig` clones onto
--             the pieces: change an id, change it in both
--   groups    each moving part and lamp: kind, pivot, axis and travel from the export
--             (Blender's axes there, turned into the game's here). Not listed: the static
--             body. The pop-up lights are modelled DOWN; their travel raises them. Each
--             tail lamp is one red lens: tail and brake lamp both. The front calipers ride
--             the steering hubs (`fixed` on `Hub*`), which is what `steer` means in the export
--   pieces    { name, mesh asset id, size x, y, z, centre x, y, z }, read off the import.
--             Every piece is turned half a turn about Y (the importer read the file facing +Z)
--   points    the driver's seat (right-hand drive), where the driver steps out (the
--             export's estimate) and the nine lowest points of the underside
--
-- The meshes and maps were uploaded by the user's import; they are this game's own art. The
-- wheels' maps came with the downloaded model the restyle started from: its source and
-- licence are still to be recorded before it ships (`assets/car-ae86/MANIFEST.md`).

